WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.930
ANTHONY MAYS: Hi,
I'm Anthony D. Mays--

00:00:06.930 --> 00:00:08.140
software engineer at Google.

00:00:08.140 --> 00:00:11.170
And I'm here to talk to
you on the topic of one

00:00:11.170 --> 00:00:14.708
to more-- why sharing
our narrative matters.

00:00:14.708 --> 00:00:16.000
Again, my name is Anthony Mays.

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.125
I'm a software engineer at
Google-- just a software

00:00:18.125 --> 00:00:22.180
engineer, but I also happen
to be a DEI advocate.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:24.760
And I think it'd be
interesting to start off

00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:28.780
by just talking about
how I became a DEI

00:00:28.780 --> 00:00:31.240
advocate in the first place.

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:34.210
I started off my
life as a foster kid

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:38.770
in Compton, after being exposed
to physical and sexual abuse.

00:00:38.770 --> 00:00:42.190
And throughout that journey, I
learned how to be a programmer,

00:00:42.190 --> 00:00:45.300
and learned how to code
when I was very young.

00:00:45.300 --> 00:00:48.640
Began to become very
interested in computer science,

00:00:48.640 --> 00:00:51.510
and went to the University
of California, Irvine

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:54.528
to get a degree in that area.

00:00:54.528 --> 00:00:57.070
Went throughout my career not
thinking that I was good enough

00:00:57.070 --> 00:01:00.520
to reach a place like a Google
or one of the other big tech

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:01.520
companies.

00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:03.680
But I always wanted
to work for Google.

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:07.070
And so after failing
the interview process

00:01:07.070 --> 00:01:09.790
and then succeeding
on my second try,

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:12.400
I now find myself a Googler.

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:15.220
And it's been the culmination
of a lot of things

00:01:15.220 --> 00:01:17.440
and a dream come true for me.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:21.460
And I didn't anticipate
becoming a DEI advocate.

00:01:21.460 --> 00:01:24.350
By the way, DEI stands
for diversity, equity,

00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:26.350
and inclusion-- if you
didn't already know.

00:01:26.350 --> 00:01:28.900
And these are things that are
very important to me, which

00:01:28.900 --> 00:01:31.030
is one of the reasons
why I've become

00:01:31.030 --> 00:01:34.520
so passionate about advocating
for more diversity, equity,

00:01:34.520 --> 00:01:36.190
and inclusion in tech.

00:01:36.190 --> 00:01:39.160
Championing more belonging
in this space that

00:01:39.160 --> 00:01:40.870
has traditionally
been challenged

00:01:40.870 --> 00:01:43.330
with not having
enough representation

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:46.150
from various individuals.

00:01:46.150 --> 00:01:50.350
And so I want to talk to
today about how I do this.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:54.970
How I advocate for better
DEI through something

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:58.020
that I like to describe
as sharing your narrative.

00:01:58.020 --> 00:02:01.260
Now, there's three things
that enables me to do

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:03.270
what I do as a DEI advocate.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:06.720
First is just having a
strong company culture that

00:02:06.720 --> 00:02:09.340
enables me to do what I do.

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.330
Second is having the
support and participation

00:02:12.330 --> 00:02:17.760
of people from throughout
all levels of the company.

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:19.500
That's been very
important for me,

00:02:19.500 --> 00:02:23.100
as others have championed
and supported me in my work.

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:25.350
And then, lastly, this idea
of sharing your narrative.

00:02:25.350 --> 00:02:29.800
So I'm just going to break each
of these down to be more clear.

00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:32.970
So when we talk about having
an empowering culture,

00:02:32.970 --> 00:02:35.670
there's really four things
that I think work well

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:37.470
about the culture at Google.

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.970
First of all, the culture
is strong and well-defined.

00:02:40.970 --> 00:02:45.240
There's things that make
Google's culture unique.

00:02:45.240 --> 00:02:49.200
The people that we hire
tend to be very intelligent.

00:02:49.200 --> 00:02:50.650
Tend to be quirky.

00:02:50.650 --> 00:02:52.860
We describe it as
googliness Googliness

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.560
is a description of the
culture that we seek to have.

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:57.990
One where innovation
is encouraged.

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:02.580
Where people work hard, but
are also able to collaborate.

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:06.330
A culture where you can have
fun and enjoy what you do,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.710
but at the same time, tackle
very deep and hard and meaty

00:03:10.710 --> 00:03:11.750
problems.

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.430
And that kind of culture
is well-suited for someone

00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:17.400
who is working in the DEI
space because there are so

00:03:17.400 --> 00:03:18.740
many challenges.

00:03:18.740 --> 00:03:21.070
There's a lot of
obstacles to overcome.

00:03:21.070 --> 00:03:24.480
And having a culture that
champions and supports

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.730
the best and brightest
is important.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:29.710
As part of that, trust in
leadership is important.

00:03:29.710 --> 00:03:32.760
And I don't mean managers
and managers of managers.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:34.350
What I mean is leaders.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:38.940
People who are naturally
inclined to find a problem,

00:03:38.940 --> 00:03:41.490
and see it, and
want to tackle it.

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:44.070
You want to find people
who can be leaders,

00:03:44.070 --> 00:03:47.560
even if they don't have the
formal title-- as I don't.

00:03:47.560 --> 00:03:49.800
You want to support
them in leadership.

00:03:49.800 --> 00:03:51.690
And it's important that
when you find leaders

00:03:51.690 --> 00:03:54.720
that you trust them to be
leaders and support them

00:03:54.720 --> 00:03:56.268
in their journey.

00:03:56.268 --> 00:03:58.560
One of the other things that's
important about Google's

00:03:58.560 --> 00:04:01.050
culture is the
emphasis on results.

00:04:01.050 --> 00:04:03.600
So it's not just about
saying cool things.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.060
It's about doing cool things.

00:04:06.060 --> 00:04:09.540
And as a DEI advocate, one of
the things that is important

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.290
is that I'm held accountable for
showing the results of the work

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:14.220
that I do.

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:18.269
And making sure that we're
moving in the right direction.

00:04:18.269 --> 00:04:21.060
Measuring results
is important, too.

00:04:21.060 --> 00:04:25.920
So having regular surveys
that gauge employee sentiment,

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:29.130
and really understanding how
people are thinking and feeling

00:04:29.130 --> 00:04:31.380
is a very important part of
ensuring that the culture

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.210
remains strong.

00:04:33.210 --> 00:04:35.850
And then last, but not least,
there are enablement tools.

00:04:35.850 --> 00:04:38.310
And in order to
be a DEI advocate,

00:04:38.310 --> 00:04:40.830
there are a lot of
tools that I need.

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:43.380
Everything from scheduling
and calendering,

00:04:43.380 --> 00:04:47.610
to social media tools, to
templates, to program guides.

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:52.350
You name it, there's a lot of
tools that are used in the work

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:54.570
that I do to help
me be effective.

00:04:54.570 --> 00:04:56.880
And it's important to have
quick and ready access

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:57.920
to those tools.

00:04:57.920 --> 00:05:00.600
Oftentimes, I'm not needing
to ask for permission

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:02.250
to use these tools.

00:05:02.250 --> 00:05:05.940
I'm just encouraged to use them,
and to use them responsibly.

00:05:05.940 --> 00:05:09.180
And having that kind
of freedom enables

00:05:09.180 --> 00:05:13.020
me to be very effective
as a DEI advocate.

00:05:13.020 --> 00:05:15.240
Now in addition to the
culture, another thing

00:05:15.240 --> 00:05:17.130
that's very important
is making sure

00:05:17.130 --> 00:05:19.850
that everyone is
helping to drive change.

00:05:19.850 --> 00:05:22.830
You know, a culture it's not
just up to one or two people

00:05:22.830 --> 00:05:24.700
to decide how that
culture is going to be.

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:27.630
Every time you add a new
person to the company,

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:29.950
every time you add in the
person of the culture,

00:05:29.950 --> 00:05:32.067
it impacts the culture.

00:05:32.067 --> 00:05:33.900
And it's important that
everyone understands

00:05:33.900 --> 00:05:36.790
that they have a role and
place in this discussion

00:05:36.790 --> 00:05:38.170
and in this change.

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:40.800
It's not just up
to black people--

00:05:40.800 --> 00:05:42.900
such as myself, for instance.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:47.190
And when you don't have a
culture in which everyone feels

00:05:47.190 --> 00:05:49.380
encouraged and
empowered to participate

00:05:49.380 --> 00:05:53.610
it places the burden on those
who really seek and desire,

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.470
maybe even need
change to do the work.

00:05:56.470 --> 00:05:58.450
And that's not healthy.

00:05:58.450 --> 00:06:00.540
One of the challenges
of being me--

00:06:00.540 --> 00:06:05.190
being a person who happens
to identify as a black man

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:12.420
and as a Christian-- is that
there aren't that many of me

00:06:12.420 --> 00:06:13.470
in tech.

00:06:13.470 --> 00:06:14.890
At least, not yet.

00:06:14.890 --> 00:06:19.140
And it's unfair to place
all of the burden on change

00:06:19.140 --> 00:06:21.900
on the people who are
the least represented

00:06:21.900 --> 00:06:24.043
or who aren't well-represented.

00:06:24.043 --> 00:06:25.710
Furthermore, it's
important for everyone

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:28.260
to understand why you
want more representation.

00:06:28.260 --> 00:06:30.810
Why you want more
inclusiveness and belonging.

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:33.000
So that everyone can
buy into the same vision

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:35.500
going in the same direction.

00:06:35.500 --> 00:06:37.200
Now, one of the
things that I believe

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:40.950
is very important towards
helping us achieve this goal is

00:06:40.950 --> 00:06:42.660
the idea of the narrative.

00:06:42.660 --> 00:06:45.580
And it used to be called
diversity narrative.

00:06:45.580 --> 00:06:47.550
You can call it that
or belonging narrative.

00:06:47.550 --> 00:06:48.490
However it is.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:52.530
But the idea of the narrative
is that it is a story that

00:06:52.530 --> 00:06:54.840
fleshes out who you are.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:57.700
It really fleshes out
not just who you are,

00:06:57.700 --> 00:07:00.220
but why you care about
diversity, equity,

00:07:00.220 --> 00:07:01.840
and inclusion.

00:07:01.840 --> 00:07:05.310
And I would like to think
that the narrative reflects

00:07:05.310 --> 00:07:07.470
the things that don't
show up on your resume.

00:07:07.470 --> 00:07:09.420
A lot of us have skills
and qualifications

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:13.290
that we know make us suitable
for executing the business

00:07:13.290 --> 00:07:15.690
tasks that we are
assigned to execute.

00:07:15.690 --> 00:07:18.240
But there are other
things that inform

00:07:18.240 --> 00:07:22.260
how we are the way that we are.

00:07:22.260 --> 00:07:25.890
These are attributes
and qualities

00:07:25.890 --> 00:07:27.900
that we gain over
our lifetime that

00:07:27.900 --> 00:07:31.530
inform the technical
aspects of what we do.

00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:34.830
And it's really about, as
Martin Luther King said once,

00:07:34.830 --> 00:07:37.230
the content of your character.

00:07:37.230 --> 00:07:40.170
And we want to have a
good understanding of how

00:07:40.170 --> 00:07:42.690
our character is shaped
the way that it is,

00:07:42.690 --> 00:07:45.510
and how it is that we
relate to other people.

00:07:45.510 --> 00:07:47.670
And how the differences
that we bring,

00:07:47.670 --> 00:07:51.180
most importantly, inform
how we make decisions,

00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.150
whether in a team
or on a project,

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:57.390
so that we're able to
build one another up.

00:07:57.390 --> 00:08:00.000
And this is really
about making sure

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.630
that we've got a
culture of belonging.

00:08:03.630 --> 00:08:06.900
Where everyone feels like they
have a part in the discussion.

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:10.802
Where everyone feels like they
have a part and a role to play.

00:08:10.802 --> 00:08:13.260
And that they feel like they're
part of the team in helping

00:08:13.260 --> 00:08:16.390
to achieve the right results.

00:08:16.390 --> 00:08:19.330
In today's climate, where you
have such underrepresentation,

00:08:19.330 --> 00:08:21.640
it's common for
people like me who

00:08:21.640 --> 00:08:23.290
come from a
nontraditional background

00:08:23.290 --> 00:08:26.500
to sometimes feel
excluded or to not feel

00:08:26.500 --> 00:08:28.420
like we belong as we should.

00:08:28.420 --> 00:08:30.880
And I've talked
to many people who

00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:34.210
have had that narrative
over the years, whether it's

00:08:34.210 --> 00:08:37.190
a woman who's a
software engineer,

00:08:37.190 --> 00:08:40.630
but is treated as though
she's a project manager.

00:08:40.630 --> 00:08:46.120
Or whether it's a black
person who is misidentified.

00:08:46.120 --> 00:08:48.880
Or any of those other things.

00:08:48.880 --> 00:08:51.160
Those kinds of interactions
cause people to not

00:08:51.160 --> 00:08:53.470
feel like they belong,
and as a result of that,

00:08:53.470 --> 00:08:57.400
sometimes when we chase
diversity before inclusion,

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.010
we end up making this
revolving door effect.

00:09:00.010 --> 00:09:04.940
Where the people from diverse
backgrounds that we hire

00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:06.523
end up walking right
back out the door

00:09:06.523 --> 00:09:08.023
because they don't
feel like they're

00:09:08.023 --> 00:09:09.110
part of the organization.

00:09:09.110 --> 00:09:12.790
And so narratives can help build
more representation and more

00:09:12.790 --> 00:09:15.820
belonging, more specifically,
within the organization

00:09:15.820 --> 00:09:18.970
so that people who are hired
and new talent that comes along

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:21.040
know that they are part
of the organization.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:22.290
That they're part of the team.

00:09:22.290 --> 00:09:26.240
And that they belong and
can do productive work.

00:09:26.240 --> 00:09:27.790
And so one of the
things that I think

00:09:27.790 --> 00:09:30.370
I do more than anything
else as a DEI advocate

00:09:30.370 --> 00:09:34.700
is to empower other people
to share their narrative.

00:09:34.700 --> 00:09:36.910
To open up about
what makes them them.

00:09:36.910 --> 00:09:41.980
And how they contribute to
the culture and to the team

00:09:41.980 --> 00:09:45.340
and to doing the things that
they do in a more holistic way.

00:09:45.340 --> 00:09:49.810
And so, this is what brings
me to a special initiative

00:09:49.810 --> 00:09:52.850
that I started within Google,
and I'd like to share with you.

00:09:52.850 --> 00:09:55.100
Because I think you
may be wondering, well,

00:09:55.100 --> 00:09:56.835
how do you share your narrative?

00:09:56.835 --> 00:09:58.210
How do you encourage
other people

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:00.825
to share their narrative, and
to talk about who they are,

00:10:00.825 --> 00:10:02.200
and what they
bring to the table,

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.810
and why DEI matters to them?

00:10:04.810 --> 00:10:06.580
I think that once
you understand how

00:10:06.580 --> 00:10:08.320
to encourage other
people to do that,

00:10:08.320 --> 00:10:11.740
it can have a very powerful and
transformative effect, as we'll

00:10:11.740 --> 00:10:14.277
talk about shortly.

00:10:14.277 --> 00:10:16.360
So I started the thing
called Share Your Narrative

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:17.080
within Google.

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:21.070
And what it does is it provides
a platform and a framework

00:10:21.070 --> 00:10:25.670
and a space for people to
talk about their narratives.

00:10:25.670 --> 00:10:29.170
And I think that it's
one of those things that

00:10:29.170 --> 00:10:33.850
really helps to scale my
impact as a DEI advocate.

00:10:33.850 --> 00:10:36.130
Because I'm just
one person, and I

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:39.640
want to have as much impact as
widespread as possible while

00:10:39.640 --> 00:10:40.720
I'm sitting in my seat.

00:10:40.720 --> 00:10:42.970
And I believe that the Share
Your Narrative initiative

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.190
has helped me to do that.

00:10:45.190 --> 00:10:48.340
As I mentioned before, the
Share Your Narrative initiative

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:50.000
really is three components.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:54.790
One is a platform
that is built and used

00:10:54.790 --> 00:10:57.530
in order to encourage
people to, first of all,

00:10:57.530 --> 00:11:01.550
understand what a narrative is,
and then, two, how to share it.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:02.860
Two, there's a framework.

00:11:02.860 --> 00:11:05.750
And so there's specific things
that make up a good narrative,

00:11:05.750 --> 00:11:07.747
and we talk about that
And then, lastly, we

00:11:07.747 --> 00:11:09.580
provide an environment
where you can explore

00:11:09.580 --> 00:11:14.560
what that narrative is, and
understand how it works,

00:11:14.560 --> 00:11:17.870
and how you can improve
it and make it better.

00:11:17.870 --> 00:11:19.810
So first thing, the platform.

00:11:19.810 --> 00:11:22.750
When I built the Share
Your Narrative site,

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:23.660
it was very simple.

00:11:23.660 --> 00:11:26.020
It was just a Google Sites site.

00:11:26.020 --> 00:11:29.210
And it linked to a
Google+ community,

00:11:29.210 --> 00:11:31.120
which is now Google Currents.

00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:33.160
And that's really all I needed.

00:11:33.160 --> 00:11:35.020
Google Currents was
great for providing

00:11:35.020 --> 00:11:39.580
a place where folks could
post video, articles, images.

00:11:39.580 --> 00:11:42.430
As they're sharing a
story from their life,

00:11:42.430 --> 00:11:44.920
they're able to show a
picture of maybe that time

00:11:44.920 --> 00:11:46.940
of their life to
help folks visualize

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:49.910
what's going on or maybe
pointing to a helpful video.

00:11:49.910 --> 00:11:52.690
Having rich multimedia on
a social media platform

00:11:52.690 --> 00:11:54.670
is very important
for helping people

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:56.500
to really share
parts of themselves

00:11:56.500 --> 00:11:58.600
that maybe they
wouldn't otherwise

00:11:58.600 --> 00:12:01.780
share if they didn't have
that kind of a platform.

00:12:01.780 --> 00:12:04.870
On the website that we have
for Share Your Narrative,

00:12:04.870 --> 00:12:06.060
it's just a static page.

00:12:06.060 --> 00:12:09.070
It includes some
instructions and FAQ.

00:12:09.070 --> 00:12:13.690
Really very simple, but
it's an one-stop shop

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:16.360
for finding out what a narrative
is and how to share it.

00:12:16.360 --> 00:12:20.320
And at Google, we make it
very easy to share websites.

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:21.800
We have special links.

00:12:21.800 --> 00:12:24.670
And so I crafted a special
link that points to the site

00:12:24.670 --> 00:12:28.870
and made it very easy and
memorable for people to get to.

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:30.320
Next comes the framework.

00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:33.700
And I'll admit that I didn't
invent the diversity narrative

00:12:33.700 --> 00:12:37.760
framework, but more
than hardly use it.

00:12:37.760 --> 00:12:41.690
Now a diversity narrative
really is formed of three parts.

00:12:41.690 --> 00:12:45.470
First, there is the reason
why you care about DEI.

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:48.070
And so you talk about,
what are the things that

00:12:48.070 --> 00:12:52.000
are meaningful to you about
improving representation

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:54.590
or more belonging
or those things,

00:12:54.590 --> 00:12:57.530
and you'll just briefly
touch on that theme.

00:12:57.530 --> 00:12:59.890
And the theme will set up
for the second part, which

00:12:59.890 --> 00:13:02.560
is a story that
helps illustrate why

00:13:02.560 --> 00:13:05.600
you care about that aspect
of diversity, equity,

00:13:05.600 --> 00:13:06.580
and inclusion.

00:13:06.580 --> 00:13:08.990
It should be something real,
something that happened.

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:11.643
It can be something that
relates to a business situation

00:13:11.643 --> 00:13:13.060
that you've
encountered, where you

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:18.210
saw an optimal result achieved
because of DEI or the opposite,

00:13:18.210 --> 00:13:22.970
if the lack of representation
didn't result in the outcome

00:13:22.970 --> 00:13:24.420
that you were hoping for.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:26.240
So there's that story.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:29.092
And if there isn't a story that
you feel comfortable sharing

00:13:29.092 --> 00:13:30.800
about yourself, perhaps
there's something

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:34.100
that you've noticed in another
situation that didn't directly

00:13:34.100 --> 00:13:35.780
happen to you that
made you think

00:13:35.780 --> 00:13:37.940
about why DEI is important.

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:43.610
Certainly, the events
pertaining to George Floyd

00:13:43.610 --> 00:13:46.580
has caused many to not
just hear about, but also

00:13:46.580 --> 00:13:51.260
see some of the reasons
why this work is

00:13:51.260 --> 00:13:54.040
important and impactful.

00:13:54.040 --> 00:13:57.230
And so you can talk about that
in your narrative and share,

00:13:57.230 --> 00:13:59.960
maybe, a moment from
your life where it really

00:13:59.960 --> 00:14:01.483
hit home for you.

00:14:01.483 --> 00:14:02.900
And then, the last
thing that I'll

00:14:02.900 --> 00:14:05.130
talk about in the framework
is a call to action.

00:14:05.130 --> 00:14:06.840
So you'll have the purpose--

00:14:06.840 --> 00:14:07.970
why DEI matters to you.

00:14:07.970 --> 00:14:10.290
You'll have the story that
connects to that purpose.

00:14:10.290 --> 00:14:12.530
And then, lastly,
some call to action.

00:14:12.530 --> 00:14:14.987
And the call to action
is super important.

00:14:14.987 --> 00:14:16.820
In the call to action,
you want to make sure

00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:19.790
that you're inviting people
to join you in the work.

00:14:19.790 --> 00:14:22.220
Because hopefully,
in parts one and two,

00:14:22.220 --> 00:14:26.270
you've inspired people to
understand why they, too,

00:14:26.270 --> 00:14:28.100
should care about DEI.

00:14:28.100 --> 00:14:30.890
And if they're ready to
act, you can provide them

00:14:30.890 --> 00:14:33.750
a way to join you in the work.

00:14:33.750 --> 00:14:37.410
The last thing that the Share
Your Narrative community does

00:14:37.410 --> 00:14:40.740
is provide a safe
environment for people

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:43.710
to share their narrative
and to feel supported.

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:46.030
And so it manifests in two ways.

00:14:46.030 --> 00:14:48.300
Number one, there's
someone who reviews

00:14:48.300 --> 00:14:50.670
the narratives for
spelling and grammar

00:14:50.670 --> 00:14:52.110
and just to make
sure that things

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:54.870
are cohesive and succinct.

00:14:54.870 --> 00:14:58.470
Then once someone decides to
post within the Google Currents

00:14:58.470 --> 00:15:03.000
community, other people can
like on those narratives

00:15:03.000 --> 00:15:05.730
or share their
comments or support

00:15:05.730 --> 00:15:07.040
the people who are writing.

00:15:07.040 --> 00:15:09.990
And from what I've seen,
it's always been positive.

00:15:09.990 --> 00:15:13.710
It's always been good when
people post these narratives

00:15:13.710 --> 00:15:15.960
and are able to say,
hey, I connect with that.

00:15:15.960 --> 00:15:18.900
One of the other unique things
about specifically using

00:15:18.900 --> 00:15:20.590
Google Currents is
that in our community

00:15:20.590 --> 00:15:24.400
we're able to sort posts by a
number of different factors.

00:15:24.400 --> 00:15:26.760
And so if you were
writing a post that

00:15:26.760 --> 00:15:29.400
focused on why age
representation matters,

00:15:29.400 --> 00:15:31.080
you could categorize
your post that way.

00:15:31.080 --> 00:15:33.240
Or if you're talking
about race or gender,

00:15:33.240 --> 00:15:34.470
you could categorize those.

00:15:34.470 --> 00:15:36.330
And then it gives the
community a great way

00:15:36.330 --> 00:15:40.260
to be able to see the narratives
from these various axes

00:15:40.260 --> 00:15:43.820
of diversity that
one may care about.

00:15:43.820 --> 00:15:46.460
Now our community
is rather small.

00:15:46.460 --> 00:15:50.000
It's just 156 community
members and growing.

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:53.130
And I'd say that's not bad
for a software engineer.

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:55.930
But if those numbers
seem small, I

00:15:55.930 --> 00:15:59.390
want you to think about
the multiplicative effect

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:01.170
of sharing your narrative.

00:16:01.170 --> 00:16:04.290
See, a narrative isn't just
shared within our community.

00:16:04.290 --> 00:16:08.330
Oftentimes, it can be
included in an email link.

00:16:08.330 --> 00:16:10.500
It can be shared
during a team meeting.

00:16:10.500 --> 00:16:13.160
It can be spoken in an offsite.

00:16:13.160 --> 00:16:16.890
Used during a speech before a
very technical presentation.

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:18.890
There's any number of
ways that you can do that.

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:21.650
Even just sharing with
your own family or friends.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:25.490
By sharing your narrative,
you connect with other people

00:16:25.490 --> 00:16:27.870
and help them to understand
why it matters to you.

00:16:27.870 --> 00:16:30.260
And if they care
about you, why it

00:16:30.260 --> 00:16:32.520
should matter to them, as well.

00:16:32.520 --> 00:16:35.390
And so, the narrative has
a multiplicative effect.

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:38.300
My own story about my journey
to Google from Compton

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:42.440
was featured in a BuzzFeed video
that had over 5 million views.

00:16:42.440 --> 00:16:45.045
And certainly, it's
probably safe to say

00:16:45.045 --> 00:16:47.420
that hundreds of thousands of
people all around the world

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:48.980
have heard my story
and narrative,

00:16:48.980 --> 00:16:50.990
and have been encouraged
by my journey,

00:16:50.990 --> 00:16:54.200
as I've given of myself
to talk about how it is

00:16:54.200 --> 00:16:56.630
that I came to be in the space.

00:16:56.630 --> 00:16:59.340
And how I encourage other
people to do the same.

00:16:59.340 --> 00:17:02.920
And so there is this
multiplicative effect of each

00:17:02.920 --> 00:17:04.670
and every single one
of those individuals.

00:17:04.670 --> 00:17:07.319
Some of who are
engineers, like myself.

00:17:07.319 --> 00:17:09.950
Others are executives,
some are in sales.

00:17:09.950 --> 00:17:11.960
They come from all
different parts of Google

00:17:11.960 --> 00:17:13.490
and all around the
world, and they

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:14.810
participate in this community.

00:17:14.810 --> 00:17:17.300
And also share their
own narratives in places

00:17:17.300 --> 00:17:19.500
that we may not be able to see.

00:17:19.500 --> 00:17:21.500
And so there's a
great positive benefit

00:17:21.500 --> 00:17:24.440
of being able to see
these narratives grow

00:17:24.440 --> 00:17:27.069
and be shared widely.

00:17:27.069 --> 00:17:29.860
This really is
about scalability.

00:17:29.860 --> 00:17:33.160
There's only so much that
I can do as an individual.

00:17:33.160 --> 00:17:36.010
But I want to make sure that I
can empower the next generation

00:17:36.010 --> 00:17:38.830
to do what I'm doing and to
do it even better so that we

00:17:38.830 --> 00:17:43.510
can encourage more inclusiveness
and belonging in the space.

00:17:43.510 --> 00:17:46.020
Now I just want to end with
a couple of final thoughts

00:17:46.020 --> 00:17:48.070
that I think are important.

00:17:48.070 --> 00:17:51.660
First of all, understand
that knowledge isn't power.

00:17:51.660 --> 00:17:53.640
Not knowledge by itself.

00:17:53.640 --> 00:17:56.430
Knowledge rightly
applied is powerful.

00:17:56.430 --> 00:17:58.080
And that's really
what this is about.

00:17:58.080 --> 00:18:00.930
A lot of us have been learning
over the past few months

00:18:00.930 --> 00:18:07.500
about why the conversations
about race, about inclusion,

00:18:07.500 --> 00:18:09.220
about belonging--

00:18:09.220 --> 00:18:10.890
not just the tech,
but in the world--

00:18:10.890 --> 00:18:12.630
why these things matter.

00:18:12.630 --> 00:18:15.270
It's not enough just to
sit on that knowledge

00:18:15.270 --> 00:18:16.420
and not do anything.

00:18:16.420 --> 00:18:18.785
It's important to do something.

00:18:18.785 --> 00:18:20.160
And sharing your
narrative can be

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:25.080
one of the easiest ways for
you to be impactful in terms

00:18:25.080 --> 00:18:27.750
of inviting other people
to the conversation,

00:18:27.750 --> 00:18:29.880
inviting other
people to the work.

00:18:29.880 --> 00:18:32.490
Now even though it's
one of the easiest ways,

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:34.720
it's still not easy.

00:18:34.720 --> 00:18:37.830
And I know that it takes you
sharing a bit of yourself

00:18:37.830 --> 00:18:42.120
that maybe you didn't plan on
sharing at work, and that's OK.

00:18:42.120 --> 00:18:43.410
You're not alone.

00:18:43.410 --> 00:18:45.360
And one of the best
things about the Share

00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.030
Your Narrative program is
that it has shown people

00:18:48.030 --> 00:18:49.690
that they're not alone.

00:18:49.690 --> 00:18:52.620
I'm not alone as a
person who has struggled

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:54.690
through a rough upbringing
to become a software

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:56.190
engineer, beating the odds.

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:58.560
There are other people
like me across all

00:18:58.560 --> 00:19:01.020
different backgrounds
and all different shades.

00:19:01.020 --> 00:19:03.480
And that's really what
belonging is all about.

00:19:03.480 --> 00:19:05.850
At the end of the
day, I know that,

00:19:05.850 --> 00:19:08.130
as a result of this
program and other things,

00:19:08.130 --> 00:19:10.230
that I belong just as
much as anyone else,

00:19:10.230 --> 00:19:11.990
because I'm a Googler.

00:19:11.990 --> 00:19:15.370
And so, I hope that this time
has been encouraging to you.

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:18.050
Thank you for tuning in to
watch this presentation.

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:20.370
And the best of
luck to you as you

00:19:20.370 --> 00:19:22.750
pursue greater
diversity, equity,

00:19:22.750 --> 00:19:25.590
and inclusion in your
company or organization.

00:19:25.590 --> 00:19:27.380
Thank you.

